6. *Jev runner binding + the condense firing form (firing 17, applied):* **(a) Runner binding.** The three Jev call files' call-command passages still carry their pre-firing conditional text — jev-prewave.md:29 reads "BINDING OWED AT FIRST FIRING" and requires the exact runner command recorded there; the observe-exit and plan-exit call commands retain the same conditional form — despite 17 Jev firings this cycle (8 gates + 9 prewave) and firings since Cycle 026. The binding EXISTS in practice, on the seat's report: the seat reports every Jev firing ran the fleet standard one-stream route — payload size+tail-verified, then `cat <payload> | ~/workspace/codex-local/node_modules/.bin/codex exec - > <return>.raw 2>&1` with no stdin field (the Cycle 035 §9.1 standard). The payload/return pairs on disk evidence the firings and their contents; they do not by themselves verify the exact shell invocation of each firing or that the route satisfied a sealed decision-model transport — the route is therefore labeled SEAT-REPORTED, and no stronger claim is made for it. What is stale is the guard TEXT, which was never updated to record the binding it demands. Disposition: the three passages are named **NOT-YET-AMENDED**, consumer the next Condense that edits guard files (or HQ's candidate pass), reason as for the stale firing-state texts (a guard-text edit cycle re-opens this review on changed definitions; the staleness is priced and owned). They extend the INCOMPLETE stamp's basis (§9 stamp, corrected above). The standing board charge for a deterministic Jev transport binding (`rec-2026-11-15-jev-binding`, still "sent" on the video-digestion board) is the same debt at board level and is not discharged by this record. **(b) Condense firing form.** The codex-condense feeding contract says "One run per cycle," while its review section prescribes that a REVISE verdict "returns the named amendment to Condense for re-encoding before the cycle closes." Re-encoding after REVISE does not e